_____________________________________________
Overall Structure (MVC)
_____________________________________________
The Model (PaintModel) is responsible for storing all application state. It holds the list of shapes drawn on the canvas,
the currently selected shape, clipboard data for copy/paste, the active preview shape, and drawing settings such as color,
thickness, and fill mode. It also manages undo and redo using two stacks of Command objects. All changes to the application’s
state occur through the model, making it the single source of truth.

The View contains everything the user interacts with visually. The PaintPanel is the main drawing surface, and its only
job is to render whatever the model contains. It observes the model using Java’s Observable/Observer framework: whenever
the model changes, PaintPanel automatically redraws the canvas. Supporting UI panels include ShapeChooserPanel, ColorChooserPanel,
FillStyleChooserPanel, EditToolPanel, and ThicknessChooserPanel. These panels allow the user to adjust settings and trigger actions,
and they update the model accordingly.

The Controller role is handled by the tool system. Instead of the view containing large switch statements or manually interpreting
mouse events, each Tool encapsulates a specific behavior, making the system modular and easier to reason about.

_____________________________________________
Tool System (Strategy Pattern + ToolFactory)
_____________________________________________
A major architectural improvement in this project was the introduction of a proper Tool system. Each drawing or editing
behavior—creating circles, rectangles, squares, triangles, ovals, polylines, squiggles, selecting and moving shapes, bucket fill,
eyedropper sampling, and text placement—is implemented inside its own class.

Each tool implements the same Tool interface with the following methods:
-onPress(MouseEvent e)
-onDrag(MouseEvent e)
-onRelease(MouseEvent e)
-onMove(MouseEvent e)

This design applies the Strategy pattern: instead of PaintPanel knowing how to handle every mode, it simply forwards events
to the currently active tool. This keeps the drawing logic highly modular.

The ToolFactory creates each tool based on a ToolType enum. Whenever the user clicks a button or presses a keyboard shortcut,
the View switches the active tool by asking the factory for the appropriate instance. Adding a new tool now requires minimal
modification—just a new class and a ToolType entry.

_____________________________________________
Undo/Redo System (Command Pattern)
_____________________________________________
Undo and redo are implemented using the Command pattern. Every state-changing action creates its own Command object,
which has execute() and undo() methods.
Examples include:
-CutCommand
-PasteCommand
-ClearCanvasCommand
-RecolorCommand
-MoveCommand
-AddShapeCommand
-TextCommand
-StyleCommand

The PaintModel maintains an undo stack and a redo stack. When a command executes, it’s pushed onto the undo stack.
Undo pops a command and calls undo(), while redo re-executes a previously undone command. This makes undo/redo consistent a
cross the entire application and keeps all mutating logic centralized and reversible.

_____________________________________________
Shape Hierarchy (Composite Pattern + Inheritance)
_____________________________________________
All shapes implement the Drawable interface, and most of them extend AbstractShapeDrawable, which provides shared functionality
such as color, thickness, fill status, hit-testing helpers, and translation. Concrete shape classes include:
-Circle
-Rectangle
-Square
-Oval
-Triangle
-Polyline
-Squiggle
-Text
Since everything implements Drawable, the model only needs a single ArrayList<Drawable> to store shapes. The view draws
all shapes in a simple loop without needing to know what type they are. Each shape class provides its own logic for drawing,
detecting whether a point lies inside it (for selection), and translating its position.

_____________________________________________
Observer Pattern for Updating the UI
_____________________________________________
PaintModel extends Observable, and PaintPanel implements Observer. Whenever the model changes—whether a shape is added,
moved, recolored, or replaced—the model calls:
setChanged();
notifyObservers();

and PaintPanel responds by redrawing the canvas.

This avoids the need for tools or commands to manually refresh the display. As long as they modify the model, the UI stays in sync automatically.

_____________________________________________
Clipboard and Copy/Paste Behavior
_____________________________________________
The model supports cut, copy, and paste by storing a Drawable in a clipboard variable. Copying or pasting a shape uses a helper
method to create a fresh copy with the same geometry and style. This ensures that clipboard shapes and canvas shapes do not share
internal data structures. Pasting positions the copied shape based on the user’s action and integrates cleanly with the undo/redo system.

_____________________________________________
Keyboard Shortcuts and Cursor Management
_____________________________________________
The View handles all keyboard shortcuts for tool switching and editing actions. These include shortcuts for shapes (C, R, S, T, O, Q, L, etc.)
and standard actions such as:
-Ctrl+Z (Undo)
-Ctrl+Y (Redo)
-Ctrl+C (Copy)
-Ctrl+X (Cut)
-Ctrl+V (Paste)
-Delete (Clear)

The view also changes the cursor depending on the active tool, improving clarity and usability.

_____________________________________________
Summary
_____________________________________________
The final architecture is clean, modular, and easy to extend. The model manages all application state, the view renders
that state and manages user interaction components, and the tools act as controllers that interpret mouse input.
The Strategy pattern (Tools), Command pattern (undo/redo), and Observer pattern (model–view communication) all play a visible role
in simplifying and structuring the system.





